# SVN Fix Issue

1. Obtain the issue evidence and tracker context; SVN itself is not an issue tracker.
2. Record repository and working-copy identity. Inspect normal and `--no-ignore`
   status, mixed revisions, switched or sparse paths, conflicts, externals, locks, and
   existing local changes. When access is authorized, check remote staleness with
   `svn status -u`.
3. Do not automatically update, switch, revert, clean, or otherwise normalize the
   working copy. Those operations mutate state and may merge, conflict, or destroy
   unrelated work.
4. Reproduce the failure only when safe. Do not install dependencies or run networked,
   destructive, production-facing, privileged, or unusually expensive checks without
   confirmation.
5. Trace all discoverable callers of the affected boundary and distinguish the
   supported root cause from hypotheses.
6. Implement the smallest complete local fix while preserving unrelated work. Schedule
   intended new versioned files with `svn add`; unversioned files are absent from normal
   diffs and commits.
7. Add a focused regression test when the project has a suitable test surface.
   Otherwise use the smallest repeatable verification appropriate to code,
   configuration, documentation, or data.
8. Run the focused verification and smallest relevant suite. Record exact commands,
   results, and anything not run.
9. Show status and diff for the identical explicit target set intended for commit.
   Provide the target list, property changes, external effects, lock-release behavior,
   and proposed log message. Do not use `.` when it would include unrelated paths.
10. Obtain explicit confirmation before a branch copy, commit, tracker update, upload,
   or review submission.

If an approved commit runs, return the committed revision, verify the repository log
and diff for that revision, and show that unrelated working-copy changes remain.
A successful commit need not make the entire working copy clean or single-revision.
Redact credentials, tokens, private data, authentication details, and sensitive paths
from diffs, logs, commands, and output.
